"## Summary\nMarta acquired this target quickly — going from a 15‑second starting interval to the 960‑second ceiling in just five practice days — and retention between sessions has stayed strong overall, with only one isolated start-of-day miss and no sign of an ongoing decay pattern.\n\n## Acquisition trajectory\nSession 1 (7/3) began with three screening recalls at 0s, 15s, and 30s, then a teach step and a clean climb through 15s → 22.5s → 33.75s, all recalled. Session 2 (7/4) opened with a successful recall at 0s and continued the ladder to 33.75s before a miss at 50.63s (corrected), after which Marta climbed straight back through 33.75s → 50.63s → 75.94s, all recalled. Session 3 (7/5) was a long unbroken climb — 75.94s → 113.91s → 170.86s → 256.29s → 384.43s → 576.65s — every trial recalled. By session 4 (7/6), after a start-of-day miss, Marta still reached 864.98s, and session 5 (7/7) closed by hitting the 960s ceiling. This is a fast acquisition curve: five sessions to go from base interval to ceiling, with only two misses recorded across 22 total trials.\n\n## Reset & recovery behavior\nTwo misses occurred. In session 2, the miss at 50.63s reverted the ladder to the last successful rung (33.75s); Marta recalled there, then climbed back up through 50.63s and on to 75.94s in the same session — a full recovery within one sitting. In session 4, the 0s start-of-day probe was not yet recalled (corrected); the next trial was set at 576.65s (the last successful rung from session 3), which Marta recalled, and the session went on to reach a new high of 864.98s. Both misses were followed by prompt recovery and net forward progress, with no lingering disruption to the ladder.\n\n## Interval band reached\nMarta reliably reached and recalled the 960-second interval in session 5, which is the configured ceiling for this target — meaning within-session work on this specific rung sequence is essentially complete; there is no higher rung left to test against, since 960s is the ceiling, not an open-ended reach. The last confirmed successful rung on record is 960s.\n\n## Schedule & booster state\nThe gap between sessions has been the initial 1-day spacing (firstGap=1d), consistent across all five sessions logged (7/3 through 7/7), with the next session due 7/8. Mastery requires three separate practice days that each open with a successful start-of-day recall (masteryStreak=3). Right now startStreak=1: the sequence was interrupted by the session 4 start-of-day miss and restarted with the session 5 success. No booster step has begun (boosterStep=0) — that stage only starts once mastery is reached, and Marta is not there yet.\n\n## Limitations (n=1)\nThis is a single dyad's log for one target, and it can only tune Marta's own practice — it does not settle any broader question about spaced retrieval as a method. In particular, this data cannot compare expanding versus uniform interval schedules, cannot compare errorless versus effortful correction styles, and includes no control condition or comparison arm of any kind. Improvements seen here could reflect the specific wording of the prompt, the specific memory cue (\"Lena\"), time of day, session length, fatigue, or other unrecorded factors just as easily as the spacing algorithm itself. With only two misses in the entire log, it is not possible to draw a statistically meaningful pattern about miss frequency, retention decay, or recovery speed — one more miss or one more clean streak could shift the picture considerably. Any conclusions here describe what happened with Marta on these five specific days, not what would happen with a different person, a different target, or even the same target under different conditions."
